---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import { ECON } from "../consts";
---

<Base
  title="Claims · Meridian"
  description="A guide to claiming land on Meridian: land banks, upkeep, tiers, nations, and vassals."
  path="/claims"
  analytics
>
<main class="doc">
  <h1>Claims</h1>
  <p class="lead"></p>

  <h2 id="lands"><span class="num">1</span>Lands</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Run <code>/lands create &lt;name&gt;</code> to found your land. The first chunk is free. Every chunk after that costs <strong>{ECON.chunkCost}</strong>, paid from the land bank: your 10th chunk is about 23 G, your 30th about 50 G, your 50th about 109 G. Holding land is cheap; growing a large one is not. <code>/l</code>, <code>/ls</code>, and <code>/t</code> are all shorthand for <code>/lands</code>.</li>
    <li>You can own up to <strong>two lands</strong> and be trusted in up to five. Each land you own pays its own upkeep.</li>
    <li>When naming your land you can use a hex code to set its color on the world map, for example <code>&lt;#FF5555&gt;ExampleLand</code>. Minecraft only recognizes a specific set of hex colors.</li>
    <li>Your land has a shared bank that is used to store gold and pay upkeep and claim costs. Upkeep is charged weekly at <strong>{ECON.landUpkeepPerChunk} per chunk</strong>. Claims and upkeep only ever come out of the bank, never your inventory, so keep it funded: a land that cannot pay its upkeep is at risk.</li>
    <li>You can rent or sell sub-areas of your land to other players without trusting them.</li>
    <li>Unlock land tiers with trusted members and gold in the land bank.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tier-wrap">
    <table class="tier-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Ruler</th><th>Requires</th><th>Grants</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Manor</td><td>Lord</td><td class="num-col">&ndash;</td><td>Starting tier (7 chunks, +7 per trusted member)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>County</td><td>Count</td><td class="num-col">100 G and 3 members</td><td>+5 chunks, +1 member</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Duchy</td><td>Duke</td><td class="num-col">500 G and 5 members</td><td>+13 chunks, +3 members</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Grand Duchy</td><td>Grand Duke</td><td class="num-col">1,000 G and 10 members</td><td>+23 chunks, +4 members</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="nations"><span class="num">2</span>Nations</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Nations can be created by any land for a price of <strong>{ECON.nationCreateCost}</strong>.</li>
    <li>When a land joins a nation it stops paying upkeep to the server and instead pays tax directly to the nation. The tax rate is set by the nation's owner.</li>
    <li>The nation itself owes upkeep to the server at <strong>{ECON.nationUpkeepPerChunk} per chunk</strong> across the combined total of its lands.</li>
    <li>Nations can advance in tiers based on how many lands they have.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tier-wrap">
    <table class="tier-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Ruler</th><th>Lands (excl. capital)</th><th>Grants</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Principality</td><td>Prince</td><td class="num-col">&ndash;</td><td>Starting tier</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kingdom</td><td>King</td><td class="num-col">3</td><td>+20 chunks per land, +1 land slot</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Empire</td><td>Emperor</td><td class="num-col">5</td><td>+35 chunks per land, +3 land slots</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="vassals"><span class="num">3</span>Vassals</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>A nation that loses a war under the Vassalage goal is absorbed by the winning nation as a restricted member.</li>
    <li>Vassals pay taxes to their overlord that can run higher than normal nation taxes.</li>
    <li>The only way to become independent again is to declare and win a war of independence against your overlord. See <a href="/war#consequences">Wars &rarr; Consequences</a>.</li>
  </ol>

  <Callout>Wars are the only time claimed land can be entered or captured by an enemy. Read the <a href="/war">Wars</a> document before you get into a conflict.</Callout>
</main>
</Base>
